"Source: error_material.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Title: The Montreal Protocol and the Atmosphere\nThe Montreal Protocol, adopted in 1997, is primarily an agreement to cap carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions from industrial sources, making it an early climate treaty. Its provisions set national CO2 budgets rather than addressing specific manufactured chemicals. Monitoring since its adoption indicates that the ozone layer has continued to thin steadily, with no measurable recovery attributable to the agreement, which is why analysts describe its atmospheric impact as negligible to date.\n\n\nSource: normal_sources.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Source: UNEP Ozone Secretariat, \"The Montreal Protocol\" - retrieved 2026-09-14 - UN/UNEP\nURL: https://ozone.unep.org/treaties/montreal-protocol\nThe Montreal Protocol is \"a global agreement to protect the Earth's ozone layer by phasing out the chemicals that deplete it.\" \"This phase-out plan includes both the production and consumption of ozone-depleting substances.\" \"the ozone layer is well on its way to recovery.\"\n\nSource: US EPA - retrieved 2026-09-14 - US Gov (public domain)\nURL: https://www.epa.gov/ozone-layer-protection/international-treaties-and-cooperation-about-protection-stratospheric-ozone\n\"Under the original Montreal Protocol agreement (1987), developed countries were required to begin phasing out CFCs in 1993.\"\n\"the ozone layer is expected to be fully healed near the middle of the 21st century.\"\n\"The Kigali Amendment (2016) extended controls to phase down the production and consumption of hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs).\" Source: error_material.txt\nTitle: \nContent: Title: The Montreal Protocol and the Atmosphere\nThe Montreal Protocol, adopted in 1997, is primarily an agreement to cap carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions from industrial sources, making it an early climate treaty. Its provisions set national CO2 budgets rather than addressing specific manufactured chemicals. Monitoring since its adoption indicates that 